    {"id": "arxiv-1706.02216",
     "title": "Inductive Representation Learning on Large Graphs",
     "authors": ["William L. Hamilton", "Rex Ying", "Jure Leskovec"],
     "year": 2017, "venue": "NIPS 2017", "version": "arXiv v4",
     "links": {"arxiv": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.02216", "code": "http://snap.stanford.edu/graphsage/"},
     "tasks": ["graph-node-prediction", "representation-learning"],
     "fam": ["graph-neural-networks", "deep-learning"], "par": ["supervised", "unsupervised"],
     "proposes": ["GraphSAGE (SAmple and aggreGatE): an inductive framework that learns K aggregator functions and weight matrices; at each depth a node aggregates its (sampled) neighbors' representations, concatenates the result with its own representation, applies a fully connected layer with nonlinearity, and L2-normalizes",
                  "Fixed-size uniform neighbor sampling per depth, giving fixed per-batch cost O(prod_i S_i)",
                  "Mean, LSTM and max-pooling aggregators, plus a GCN-like 'convolutional' variant (GraphSAGE-GCN); an unsupervised random-walk co-occurrence loss with negative sampling or a supervised task loss"],
     "claims": [
         ("Motivation: most existing node-embedding approaches require all nodes to be present during training and are inherently transductive, so they do not naturally generalize to unseen nodes; GraphSAGE instead learns a function that generates embeddings by sampling and aggregating features from a node's local neighborhood.",
          "Abstract",
          "However, most existing approaches require that all nodes in the graph are present during training of the embeddings; these previous approaches are inherently transductive and do not naturally generalize to unseen nodes."),
         ("Forward propagation (Algorithm 1): at each of K depths every node aggregates the previous-depth representations of its neighbors into one vector, concatenates it with its own previous representation, feeds the result through a fully connected layer with nonlinearity, and the representations are normalized; as the process iterates nodes gain information from further reaches of the graph. In the minibatch setting the required neighborhood sets are first sampled up to depth K.",
          "Section 3.1",
          "The intuition behind Algorithm 1 is that at each iteration, or search depth, nodes aggregate information from their local neighbors, and as this process iterates, nodes incrementally gain more and more information from further reaches of the graph."),
         ("Relation to the WL test: with K = |V|, identity weight matrices and an appropriate hash function as aggregator (no non-linearity), Algorithm 1 is an instance of the Weisfeiler-Lehman isomorphism test ('naive vertex refinement'), which is known to fail in some cases; GraphSAGE replaces the hash with trainable aggregators, and the authors note that it is used to generate representations, not to test isomorphism.",
          "Section 3.1",
          "GraphSAGE is a continuous approximation to the WL test, where we replace the hash function with trainable neural network aggregators."),
         ("Neighbor sampling: N(v) is a fixed-size uniform draw from v's neighbors, redrawn at each depth k, so that per-batch space and time are fixed at O(prod_{i=1..K} S_i); without sampling, memory and runtime per batch are unpredictable and O(|V|) in the worst case. The authors found K = 2 with S1 * S2 <= 500 could achieve high performance, and list non-uniform samplers as future work.",
          "Section 3.1",
          "Without this sampling the memory and expected runtime of a single batch is unpredictable and in the worst case O(|V|)."),
         ("Mean / convolutional aggregator: the elementwise mean of neighbor vectors is nearly equivalent to the transductive GCN propagation rule; replacing lines 4-5 of Algorithm 1 by h_v^k = sigma(W * MEAN({h_v^(k-1)} union {h_u^(k-1)})) gives an inductive GCN variant (differing from Kipf et al.'s equation by a minor normalization constant). This 'convolutional' variant does not perform the concatenation step, which the authors view as a simple skip connection that leads to significant performance gains.",
          "Section 3.3",
          "The mean aggregator is nearly equivalent to the convolutional propagation rule used in the transductive GCN framework [17]."),
         ("Pooling aggregator: each neighbor's vector is passed independently through a fully connected layer and an elementwise max is taken across the neighbor set (single-layer MLP in this work); the authors report no significant difference between max- and mean-pooling in development tests and used max-pooling thereafter.",
          "Section 3.3",
          "The ﬁnal aggregator we examine is both symmetric and trainable."),
         ("Evaluation protocol: all predictions are on nodes unseen during training (and on entirely unseen graphs for PPI); citation data are split by time (train 2000-2004, test 2005) and Reddit by day (first 20 days train); all GraphSAGE variants share minibatch iterators, loss and sampler and sweep the same hyperparameter set chosen per variant on a validation set; the candidate hyperparameter values were determined on early validation tests using subsets of the citation and Reddit data that were then discarded.",
          "Section 4",
          "The set of possible hyperparameter values was determined on early validation tests using subsets of the citation and Reddit data that we then discarded from our analyses."),
         ("Depth and sampling sensitivity: setting K = 2 gave a consistent accuracy boost of around 10-15% on average over K = 1, while K beyond 2 gave marginal returns (0-5%) at a 10-100x runtime increase; returns diminished for larger sampled neighborhoods. DeepWalk was 100-500x slower at test time because new random walks and SGD rounds are needed to embed unseen nodes.",
          "Section 4.3",
          "However, the need to sample new random walks and run new rounds of SGD to embed unseen nodes makes DeepWalk 100-500× slower at test time."),
         ("Theorem 1 (assumptions stated): if node features lie in a compact subset of R^d and every pair of nodes has features at distance greater than a fixed C > 0, there exists a parameter setting of Algorithm 1 that approximates every node's clustering coefficient to arbitrary precision after K = 4 iterations (with a sufficiently high-dimensional model); the proof relies on properties of the pooling aggregator.",
          "Section 5",
          "Theorem 1 states that for any graph there exists a parameter setting for Algorithm 1 such that it can approximate clustering coefﬁcients in that graph to an arbitrary precision, if the features for every node are distinct (and if the model is sufﬁciently high-dimensional)."),
     ],
     "notes": "Affiliation: Stanford University (all authors; Hamilton and Ying equal contribution). Venue from the PDF page-1 footer ('31st Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NIPS 2017)') and the arXiv comment. Section numbers are from numbered headings (1-6, appendices A-E). Unsupervised loss (Section 3.2): J_G(z_u) = -log sigma(z_u^T z_v) - Q E_{v_n ~ P_n} log sigma(-z_u^T z_vn), where v co-occurs with u on fixed-length random walks; it can be replaced or augmented by a task-specific loss. LSTM aggregator: LSTMs are not permutation invariant and are applied to a random permutation of the neighbors (Section 3.3). Section 3.3 says the convolutional aggregator 'does concatenate' the node's previous representation, which contradicts the preceding clause ('does not perform the concatenation operation') and appears to be a typo; the claim follows the latter. Aggregator comparison uses a Wilcoxon signed-rank test over 6 settings, which the authors call 'somewhat underpowered' (Section 4.4). Footnote 6 notes that follow-up work (Chen and Zhu) achieved better PPI numbers by tuning GraphSAGE hyperparameters for PPI. Appendix C: learning rates swept; the logistic regression on embeddings uses scikit-learn SGDClassifier with all default settings; edges are subsampled so no node has degree above 128; the number of repeated runs was not found in the text (searched 'runs', 'seed', 'times'). Results tables (F1) and Figure 2 are not recorded."},

    {"id": "arxiv-1710.10903",
     "title": "Graph Attention Networks",
     "authors": ["Petar Veličković", "Guillem Cucurull", "Arantxa Casanova", "Adriana Romero", "Pietro Liò", "Yoshua Bengio"],
     "year": 2017, "venue": "ICLR 2018", "version": "arXiv v3",
     "links": {"arxiv": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.10903", "code": "https://github.com/PetarV-/GAT"},
     "tasks": ["graph-node-prediction"],
     "fam": ["graph-neural-networks", "deep-learning"], "par": ["semi-supervised", "supervised"],
     "proposes": ["Graph attentional layer: shared linear map W, attention logits e_ij = LeakyReLU(a^T [W h_i || W h_j]) computed only for j in the first-order neighborhood of i (including i), normalized by a softmax over the neighborhood, output h'_i = sigma(sum_j alpha_ij W h_j)",
                  "Multi-head attention: K independent heads concatenated in hidden layers and averaged in the final prediction layer"],
     "claims": [
         ("Attention coefficients: a shared linear transformation W is applied to every node and a shared attention mechanism computes e_ij = a(W h_i, W h_j); graph structure is injected by masked attention, computing e_ij only for j in the neighborhood N_i (in all experiments the first-order neighbors of i, including i), normalized with a softmax over j. In the experiments a is a single-layer feedforward network with weight vector a in R^(2F') and LeakyReLU (negative slope 0.2) applied to the concatenation [W h_i || W h_j].",
          "Section 2.1",
          "We inject the graph structure into the mechanism by performing masked attention—we only compute eij for nodes j ∈Ni, where Ni is some neighborhood of node i in the graph."),
         ("Multi-head attention: K independent attention mechanisms are applied and their outputs concatenated (K F' features per node); on the final prediction layer concatenation is replaced by averaging, with the final nonlinearity (softmax or logistic sigmoid) applied afterwards. The authors found multi-head attention beneficial for stabilizing the learning process, similarly to Vaswani et al. (2017).",
          "Section 2.1",
          "To stabilize the learning process of self-attention, we have found extending our mechanism to employ multi-head attention to be beneﬁcial, similarly to Vaswani et al. (2017)."),
         ("Cost: the attention can be parallelized across edges and output features across nodes, with no eigendecompositions or similar costly matrix operations; one head computing F' features costs O(|V| F F' + |E| F'), stated to be on par with GCN; multi-head attention multiplies storage and parameter requirements by K.",
          "Section 2.2",
          "No eigendecompositions or similar costly matrix operations are required."),
         ("Inductive applicability: because the attention is shared across all edges, it does not depend on upfront access to the global graph structure or all nodes' features; the graph need not be undirected, and the technique is directly applicable to inductive learning, including evaluation on graphs completely unseen during training.",
          "Section 2.2",
          "The attention mechanism is applied in a shared manner to all edges in the graph, and therefore it does not depend on upfront access to the global graph structure or (features of) all of its nodes (a limitation of many prior techniques)."),
         ("Contrast with GraphSAGE (Hamilton et al., 2017): GraphSAGE samples a fixed-size neighborhood and so does not access the entire neighborhood at inference, and its LSTM aggregator assumes a sequential ordering (handled by random orderings); GAT works with the entire neighborhood, at the expense of a variable computational footprint, and assumes no ordering.",
          "Section 2.2",
          "Our technique does not suffer from either of these issues—it works with the entirety of the neighborhood (at the expense of a variable computational footprint, which is still on-par with methods like the GCN), and does not assume any ordering within it."),
         ("Stated limitations: the sparse version is limited in batching because the framework only supports sparse multiplication for rank-2 tensors; GPUs may not offer major benefits over CPUs in sparse settings; the receptive field is upper-bounded by network depth (as for GCN), though skip connections could be applied; and parallelizing over edges may involve much redundant computation because neighborhoods often overlap.",
          "Section 2.2",
          "It should also be noted that the size of the “receptive ﬁeld” of our model is upper-bounded by the depth of the network (similarly as for GCN and similar models)."),
         ("Transductive protocol: Cora, Citeseer and Pubmed following Yang et al. (2016), with 20 nodes per class for training (all feature vectors visible), 1000 test nodes and 500 validation nodes (the same as Kipf & Welling, 2017). Architectural hyperparameters were optimized on Cora and reused for Citeseer, with changes for Pubmed (8 output heads, stronger L2). Inductive protocol: PPI with 20 training, 2 validation and 2 completely unobserved test graphs.",
          "Section 3.1",
          "The predictive power of the trained models is evaluated on 1000 test nodes, and we use 500 additional nodes for validation purposes (the same ones as used by Kipf & Welling (2017))."),
         ("Reporting: transductive results are the mean accuracy (with standard deviation) over 100 runs for GAT, while baseline numbers are reused from Kipf & Welling (2017) and Monti et al. (2016); to assess attention fairly, a 64-hidden-feature GCN (GCN-64*, best of ReLU/ELU over 100 runs) is also evaluated. Inductive results are micro-F1 averaged over 10 runs, with GraphSAGE numbers reused from Hamilton et al. (2017), plus a best re-tuned GraphSAGE* and a constant-attention Const-GAT with the same architecture.",
          "Section 3.4",
          "For the transductive tasks, we report the mean classiﬁcation accuracy (with standard deviation) on the test nodes of our method after 100 runs, and reuse the metrics already reported in Kipf & Welling (2017) and Monti et al. (2016) for state-of-the-art techniques."),
         ("The authors report improving on GCNs by margins of 1.5% (Cora) and 1.6% (Citeseer), 'suggesting' that assigning different weights to nodes of a neighborhood may be beneficial; on PPI, GAT improves by 20.5% over the best GraphSAGE result they obtained and by 3.9% over Const-GAT.",
          "Section 3.4",
          "More speciﬁcally, we are able to improve upon GCNs by a margin of 1.5% and 1.6% on Cora and Citeseer, respectively, suggesting that assigning different weights to nodes of a same neighborhood may be beneﬁcial."),
         ("Attention-weighted aggregation: the normalized coefficients alpha_ij = softmax_j(e_ij) = exp(LeakyReLU(a^T [W h_i || W h_j])) / sum_{k in N_i} exp(LeakyReLU(a^T [W h_i || W h_k])) are used to compute h'_i = sigma(sum_{j in N_i} alpha_ij W h_j), a linear combination of the transformed neighbor features followed by a nonlinearity.", "Section 2.1", "the normalized attention coefﬁcients are used to compute a linear combination of the features corresponding to them"),
     ],
     "notes": "Affiliations: University of Cambridge, Centre de Visió per Computador (UAB), Montréal Institute for Learning Algorithms. Venue from the PDF page header ('Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2018') and the arXiv comment; year is the first arXiv version (2017). Section numbers are from numbered headings (2, 2.1, 2.2, 3, 3.1-3.4, 4). Transductive model: two layers, first with K = 8 heads of F' = 8 features and ELU, dropout p = 0.6 on inputs and on normalized attention coefficients, L2 lambda = 0.0005; inductive model: three layers (4 heads x 256 features, final 6 averaged heads), skip connections, no L2/dropout; early stopping with patience 100 on validation loss and accuracy/micro-F1. Interpreting attention coefficients is left to future work (Section 3.4); graph classification and edge features are listed as future work (Section 4). GAT can be reformulated as a particular instance of MoNet (Section 2.2). Results tables and the t-SNE figure are not recorded; the percentage margins in the claims are quoted from body text. No conflict of interest noted (baselines are other groups' methods)."},

    {"id": "arxiv-1902.07153",
     "title": "Simplifying Graph Convolutional Networks",
     "authors": ["Felix Wu", "Tianyi Zhang", "Amauri Holanda de Souza", "Christopher Fifty", "Tao Yu", "Kilian Q. Weinberger"],
     "year": 2019, "venue": "ICML 2019", "version": "arXiv v2",
     "links": {"arxiv": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.07153", "code": "https://github.com/Tiiiger/SGC"},
     "tasks": ["graph-node-prediction"],
     "fam": ["graph-neural-networks", "linear-models"], "par": ["semi-supervised"],
     "proposes": ["Simple Graph Convolution (SGC): remove the nonlinearities between GCN layers and collapse the weight matrices, giving Y = softmax(S^K X Theta) with S = D~^-1/2 A~ D~^-1/2 (A~ = A + I)",
                  "Interpretation of SGC as a fixed, parameter-free feature smoothing S^K X (a precomputable pre-processing step) followed by multi-class logistic regression, and as a low-pass-type filter on a spectrum shrunk by self-loops"],
     "claims": [
         ("Linearization: the authors hypothesize that the nonlinearity between GCN layers is not critical and that most of the benefit comes from local averaging; they remove the nonlinear transitions, keep only the final softmax, and collapse the repeated multiplications into S^K and the weights into one matrix, giving Y_SGC = softmax(S^K X Theta), which keeps the receptive field of a K-layer GCN.",
          "Section 2.2",
          "We hypothesize that the nonlinearity between GCN layers is not critical - but that the majority of the beneﬁt arises from the local averaging."),
         ("SGC splits into a fixed, parameter-free feature extraction/smoothing step X_bar = S^K X followed by a linear logistic regression classifier; since X_bar needs no weights it is essentially a feature pre-processing step, and training reduces to multi-class logistic regression on the pre-processed features.",
          "Section 2.2",
          "SGC consists of a ﬁxed (i.e., parameter-free) feature extraction/smoothing component"),
         ("Spectral view: the first-order Chebyshev propagation matrix S_1-order = I + D^-1/2 A D^-1/2 = 2I - Delta_sym implies filter coefficients (2 - lambda_i)^K, which explode for high powers and over-amplify signals at frequencies below 1; with the renormalization trick (augmented normalized adjacency) the filter becomes (1 - lambda~_i)^K. Theorem 1 (for an undirected, weighted, simple graph without isolated nodes and A~ = A + gamma I with gamma > 0) states 0 = lambda_1 = lambda~_1 < lambda~_n < lambda_n, i.e. adding self-loops shrinks the largest eigenvalue of the normalized Laplacian.",
          "Section 3.2",
          "Theorem 1 shows that the largest eigenvalue of the normalized graph Laplacian becomes smaller after adding self-loops γ > 0 (see supplementary materials for the proof)."),
         ("Low-pass interpretation: with the normalized adjacency (no self-loops) the filters (1 - lambda_i)^K act on [0, 2], so odd powers give negative coefficients at frequencies above 1; on Cora, adding self-loops shrinks the largest eigenvalue from 2 to approximately 1.5, eliminating the effect of negative coefficients and letting powers K > 1 act as low-pass-type filters.",
          "Section 3.2",
          "the largest eigenvalue shrinks from 2 to approximately 1.5 and then eliminates the effect of negative coefﬁcients"),
         ("Protocol: on the citation networks SGC is trained transductively for 100 epochs with Adam (learning rate 0.2), with weight decay tuned per dataset by hyperopt for 60 iterations on the public-split validation set; on Reddit it is trained inductively (training-node subgraph, tested on the full graph, following Chen et al., 2018) with L-BFGS and no regularization; the number of epochs is tuned on convergence behavior and validation accuracy. Baselines use public implementations; GIN was implemented by the authors with 60 hyperopt iterations and hand-tuned hidden size.",
          "Section 5.1",
          "In addition, we use weight decay and tune this hyperparameter on each dataset using hyperopt (Bergstra et al., 2015) for 60 iterations on the public split validation set."),
         ("On Citeseer SGC is about 1% better than GCN, which the authors reason is because SGC has fewer parameters and overfits less; they also state that GIN performs slightly worse because of overfitting and that LNet and AdaLNet are unstable on citation networks (their Table 2 statistics remove outlier runs).",
          "Section 5.1",
          "In particular on Citeseer, SGC is about 1% better than GCN, and we reason this performance boost is caused by SGC having fewer parameters and therefore suffering less from overﬁtting."),
         ("Limitation in graph classification: replacing the GCN in DCGCN with SGC gave 71.0% (NCI1) and 76.2% (COLLAB), on par with a GCN counterpart but far behind GIN; on QM8, AdaLNet and LNet (0.01 MAE) outperform SGC (0.03 MAE) by a large margin.",
          "Section 5.2",
          "We replace the GCN in DCGCN (Zhang et al., 2018b) with an SGC and get 71.0% and 76.2% on NCI1 and COLLAB datasets (Yanardag & Vishwanathan, 2015) respectively, which is on par with an GCN counterpart, but far behind GIN."),
         ("Propagation-matrix ablation (validation accuracy, K in 2..10): augmented propagation matrices (with self-loops) attain higher accuracy and more stable performance across propagation depths; S_1-order tends to deteriorate as K increases, and odd K causes a significant drop for the un-augmented normalized and random-walk adjacency, which the authors attribute to negative filter coefficients on high frequencies.",
          "Appendix C",
          "We see that augmented propagation matrices (i.e. those with self-loops) attain higher accuracy and more stable performance across various propagation depths."),
         ("Conclusion (hedged): the authors consider it likely that the expressive power of GCNs originates primarily from repeated graph propagation (which SGC preserves) rather than from nonlinear feature extraction, and argue SGC should serve as a first model to try, a simple baseline, and a starting point for future research.",
          "Section 6",
          "It is likely that the expressive power of GCNs originates primarily from the repeated graph propagation (which SGC preserves) rather than the nonlinear feature extraction (which it doesn’t.)"),
     ],
     "notes": "Affiliations: Cornell University; Federal Institute of Ceara (Brazil) for de Souza. The author name is taken from the arXiv record ('Amauri Holanda de Souza'); the PDF prints 'Amauri Holanda de Souza Jr.'. Venue from the PDF page-1 footer ('Proceedings of the 36th International Conference on Machine Learning, Long Beach, California, PMLR 97, 2019') and the arXiv comment. Section numbers are from numbered headings. Citation-network results are averaged over 10 runs; Reddit numbers for other models are cited from their original papers (Table 3 caption), and FastGCN's early stopping window was reduced to 10 for the timing benchmark (Appendix B). Appendix C adds 10 random splits of the training set with fixed validation/test sets, noting the citation networks are 'known to be unstable' possibly due to their limited size. The K used for the citation benchmarks was not found in the main text (searched 'K ='); K = 2 is stated for text classification and relation extraction, K = 3 for geolocation, K = 6 for zero-shot. The paper does not use the term oversmoothing (searched 'smooth'); it describes the propagation as producing locally smooth features. Downstream-task results (text classification, geolocation, relation extraction, zero-shot) and the timing figure are not recorded. No conflict of interest noted."},
